#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124126 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "5c276425-f5ef-585f-9ab3-1b0262fff08e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12456" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2\\,$ on the ellipse $x^2 +4y^2-4=0$." :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint\n\n$$\ng(x,y)= x^2 +4y^2 -4=0\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= λ∇g(x, y) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y), g_{y}(x, y)⟩ \\\\\n⟨2x, 2y⟩ &= λ⟨2x, 8y⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{2x = 2λx; 2y = 8λy⇒{x(1 - λ) = 0; y(4λ - 1) = 0\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4 =0$ implies\n$4y^{2} - 4|= 0⇒y = ± 1$.\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,1 \\right), \\left(0,-1 \\right)$.\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n$y(4(1) - 1) = 0⇒y = 0$.\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4 =0$ implies\n$x^{2} - 4|= 0⇒x = ± 2$.\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(-2,0 \\right), \\left(2,0 \\right)$.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n$(0, \\pm 1), \\qquad (\\pm 2,0)$.\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 1)$ | $(\\pm 2, 0)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 1)$, and its minimum value is $1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124339 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/id #uuid "dc39fdca-dfbc-5806-b0aa-8480ee44b10f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8689" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x+y\\,$ on the ellipse $x^2 + 2y^2 =6$." :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint\n\n$$\ng(x,y)= x^2 + 2y^2 = 6\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= λ∇g(x, y) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y), g_{y}(x, y)⟩ \\\\\n⟨1, 1⟩ &= λ⟨2x, 4y⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming\n\n$$\n\\lambda\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n{1 = 2λx; 1 = 4λy⇒{x = \\frac{1}{2λ}; y = \\frac{1}{4λ}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y) = 6$ and solve for $\\lambda$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{2λ})^{2} + 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{4λ})^{2} &= 6 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4λ^{2}} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{16λ^{2}} &= 6 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4λ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{8λ^{2}} &= 6 \\\\\n\\frac{2}{8λ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{8λ^{2}} &= 6 \\\\\n\\frac{2 + 1}{8λ^{2}} &= 6 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= \\frac{1}{16} \\\\\nλ &= ± \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y$. This gives\n\n$$\nx= \\pm 2 \\qquad y = \\pm 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, -1 \\right), \\quad \\left(2, 1 \\right)\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(-2, -1\\right) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(2, 1 \\right) \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, f(x,y) \\,$ | $\\, -3 \\,$ | $\\, 3 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(2, 1 \\right)\n$$\n\nand its maximum value is $3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124342 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ebdcad27-1ee2-57e7-9ebf-29ccf0a8ad12" :question/math-academy-id "e-8736" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z) = x+y+z$ on the ellipsoid $x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36$." :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = 36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= λ∇g(x, y, z) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y, z), f_{y}(x, y, z), f_{z}(x, y, z)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y, z), g_{y}(x, y, z), g_{z}(x, y, z)⟩ \\\\\n⟨1, 1, 1⟩ &= λ⟨2x, 2y, 8z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming\n\n$$\n\\lambda \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n{1 = 2λx; 1 = 2λy; 1 = 8λz\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\, y$ and $z$ into the condition\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z) = 36\n$$\n\nand solve for $\\lambda$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 4z^{2} &= 36 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4λ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{4λ^{2}} + 4(\\frac{1}{64λ^{2}}) &= 36 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4λ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{4λ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{16λ^{2}} &= 36 \\\\\n\\frac{9}{16λ^{2}} &= 36 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= \\frac{1}{64} \\\\\nλ &= ± \\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\, y$ and $z$. This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 4 \\qquad y = \\pm 4 \\qquad z= \\pm 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n$(-4, -4, -1), \\qquad (4, 4, 1)$.\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, (-4, -4, -1) \\,$ | $\\, (4, 4, 1) \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, f(x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, -9 \\,$ | $\\, 9 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4, 4, 1)$, and its maximum value is $9$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0aa7e37-9b21-548f-9632-dff256bd29a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121430} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe7c4e9-94ea-5933-b76c-cca336dede96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121431} {:answer/id #uuid "1c0d9444-d90f-54f6-b008-9c96f984608a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121432} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6099f0-6d05-5525-8c9e-5469786edb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121433} {:answer/id #uuid "78c092c7-aa5e-5274-af00-06a2c60d318f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78c092c7-aa5e-5274-af00-06a2c60d318f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121434} :answer-field/id #uuid "d14f7371-c123-5b1d-b8c4-55e78dd983ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121429}] :question/id #uuid "83f506ab-232e-59b7-85e6-ab9697a12cb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162762" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} + (y + 2)^{2}$ on the ellipse $x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 2 = 0$."}]}
